    "answer": "Calculate revenue after discounts and refunds, then subtract variable costs such as product, fulfilment and payment fees. If an order contributes 40 before advertising, 40 is the approximate break-even acquisition cost before other costs.",
    "content": "Compare campaign cost with lead quality, margin, and the revenue campaigns generate. Start with margin Calculate revenue after discounts and refunds, then subtract variable costs such as product, fulfilment and payment fees. If an order contributes 40 before advertising, 40 is the approximate break-even acquisition cost before other costs. Include management and creative costs when assessing the programme. Follow a lead through to a sale For a lead campaign, estimate the share that qualifies and the share of qualified leads that buys. If ten leads cost 250 and one customer contributes 400 before marketing, the campaign has 150 left before overhead. Small samples can swing sharply, so review a longer period. Compare reported returns with actual orders Reconcile platform reports with transaction IDs and CRM outcomes. Distinguish new from returning customers and delayed purchases from immediate ones. Adjust attribution windows and refund treatment consistently before comparing campaigns."
